# 1. Purpose

The Diagnostics System helps Aura explain problems, verify integrations, measure health and guide users toward recovery.

Diagnostics must make technical failures understandable without forcing users to interpret network protocols, framework errors, vendor response codes or implementation details.

The user should first see:

- What happened
- What is affected
- Whether Aura is still usable
- What Aura is already doing to recover
- The next recommended action

Advanced technical information must remain available for troubleshooting, development and support, but it must never dominate the normal user experience.

---

# 2. Product Goals

The Diagnostics System shall:

- Detect and summarize integration health
- Distinguish transient problems from persistent failures
- Explain failures in human language
- Suggest the safest next action
- Provide one-tap tests where useful
- Collect privacy-safe operational history
- Support per-device and platform-wide diagnostics
- Correlate scene, automation, discovery and plugin failures
- Help developers reproduce problems
- Export sanitized reports with explicit user action
- Avoid exposing credentials, private home data or raw secrets
- Remain available when one or more integrations are offline
- Support local-first operation

---

# 3. Non-Goals

Version 1 does not aim to:

- Replace professional network-analysis tools
- Expose unrestricted packet captures
- Upload logs automatically
- Diagnose unsupported third-party infrastructure in full detail
- Guarantee repair of vendor firmware or router problems
- Show raw stack traces to normal users
- Store permanent behavioral history
- Collect viewing, presence or room-activity analytics
- Execute destructive recovery actions without confirmation
- Pretend that every failure can be uniquely identified

When uncertainty remains, Aura must say so clearly.

---

# 4. Core Principles

## 4.1 Explain Before Exposing

The first diagnostic layer is always user-facing and actionable.

Technical details belong behind an Advanced section.

## 4.2 Honest State

Aura must distinguish:

- Confirmed failure
- Suspected failure
- Temporary unavailability
- Stale data
- Unsupported capability
- Permission restriction
- Unknown state

The interface must never present an assumption as a confirmed fact.

## 4.3 Recovery First

Diagnostics should attempt safe recovery before asking the user to intervene.

Examples:

- Retry a safe idempotent request
- Reconnect an event stream
- Refresh authorization state
- Rediscover a known device by stable identity
- Refresh stale capability data

## 4.4 Local and Private

Diagnostic data stays local unless the user explicitly exports it.

## 4.5 Cross-System Correlation

A single user-visible failure may involve multiple modules.

Example:

```text
Movie Night failed
├── Philips TV did not wake
├── Apple TV woke successfully
└── Hue scene completed successfully
```

The Diagnostics System must preserve this structure rather than reducing the run to a generic failure.

