# 23. Recovery Safety

```swift
enum RecoverySafety: Sendable, Equatable {
    case safe
    case cautious
    case destructive
}
```

Examples:

- Safe: refresh state, reconnect event stream, targeted rediscovery
- Cautious: reauthenticate, restart integration
- Destructive: forget device, clear pairing, reset local mappings

Destructive actions must require explicit confirmation and describe what will be lost.

---

# 24. Automatic Recovery

Aura may perform safe automatic recovery when:

- The operation is idempotent
- User intent is preserved
- Duplicate side effects are unlikely
- The action does not alter configuration
- The retry count is bounded

Automatic recovery must stop when:

- Authentication is rejected
- Permission is denied
- The device identity is uncertain
- Repeated retries fail
- The operation may already have succeeded with side effects

---

# 25. Recovery Sequence

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Detect[Issue detected] --> Classify[Classify issue]
    Classify --> Safe{Safe automatic recovery?}
    Safe -->|Yes| Recover[Attempt bounded recovery]
    Recover --> Verify[Verify result]
    Verify -->|Success| Resolved[Mark resolved]
    Verify -->|Failure| Suggest[Present next action]
    Safe -->|No| Suggest
    Suggest --> User[User chooses action]
    User --> Execute[Execute recovery]
    Execute --> Verify
```

---

# 26. Correlation IDs

Every important operation must use a correlation ID.

Examples:

- Scene execution
- Automation execution
- Pairing attempt
- Discovery session
- Manual diagnostic test
- Device command

Correlation IDs allow Aura to connect user intent, plugin behavior, recovery and final outcome without storing unnecessary personal data.

